

SPACE‑TIME‑REALITY ENGINE: A Unified Differential Framework for Geometry, Dynamics, and Event Verification

C. Matthew BlackwellLafayette, Louisiana, USA

Abstract

The SPACE‑TIME‑REALITY Engine is a tri‑layer computational architecture that unifies encrypted spatio‑temporal geometry, interacting dynamical state machines, and cryptographically verified event logging into a single governing evolution equation. The system ingests multi‑domain data, transforms it into curvature tensors, evolves reality nodes through coupled differential operators, and records verified events in an immutable ledger. This manuscript presents the governing equation, subsystem definitions, methods, simulation protocol, and implications for computable geometry and autonomous verification systems. The framework establishes a foundation for multi‑domain simulation engines capable of evolving geometry, behavior, and verified events under one continuous mathematical operator.

1. Introduction

Computational systems traditionally treat geometry, dynamics, and verification as separate domains. Geometry is handled by physical models, dynamical behavior by simulation engines, and verification by cryptographic or logging systems. The SPACE‑TIME‑REALITY Engine integrates these domains into a unified mathematical structure.

The engine consists of three coupled layers:

Encrypted Geometry Layer — transforms multi‑domain data into curvature tensors.

Reality Node Dynamics Layer — evolves interacting state machines through differential operators.

Immutable Ledger Layer — records verified events using cryptographic hashing.

These layers evolve together under a single differential operator, enabling a unified simulation of geometry, behavior, and verified events.

